            <ArticleTitle>Terahertz-Range Polar Modes in Domain-Engineered BiFeO_{3}.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>057604</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1103/PhysRevLett.119.057604</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The dielectric permittivity and properties of electrically active lattice resonances in nanotwinned BiFeO_{3} crystals have been studied theoretically using an earlier established interatomic potential. The results suggest that an array of 71° domain walls with about 2-5 nm spacing enhances the static permittivity of BiFeO_{3} by more than an order of magnitude. This enhancement is associated with an electrically active excitation, corresponding to a collective vibration of pinned domain walls at a remarkably high frequency of about 0.3 THz.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
